241
00:36:44.400 --> 00:36:54.159
Etan (Nimbus): Today, at the end of every transaction, we credit the fee recipient of the block with the priority fee.

242
00:36:54.830 --> 00:37:05.660
Etan (Nimbus): And the proposal is to, collect all these priority fees and credit them at the end of the block, instead of after every individual transaction.

243
00:37:06.030 --> 00:37:19.170
Etan (Nimbus): That has two benefits. One of them is that, like, parallel transaction execution is a tiny bit better, because not every transaction has to write to the fee-based VPN.

244
00:37:19.500 --> 00:37:30.610
Etan (Nimbus): And also, it is better for the UX, because if you are a blog producer, and you are, like, a fee recipient, someone

245
00:37:30.870 --> 00:37:47.359
Etan (Nimbus): and you open that up in MetaMask, you have, like, 300 entries with, like, microtransactions giving you tiny priority fees in there, if you chose these, even. But, like, it… consolidating them into a single entry.

246
00:37:47.510 --> 00:37:58.310
Etan (Nimbus): would make that a little bit cleaner. The downside is that the priority fee would be only spendable in the next block.

247
00:37:58.600 --> 00:38:03.189
Etan (Nimbus): Because it would be credited at the end of the block, so that's the difference there.

248
00:38:03.950 --> 00:38:06.109
Etan (Nimbus): That's the 8115.

249
00:38:07.260 --> 00:38:19.499
