Hadrien Croubois (OpenZeppelin): Deploy some piece of code, either the code nor the proxy. And that means you have to copy all the code data to memory, and then you have to delegate, and this has a significant cost.

320
00:48:25.430 --> 00:48:40.200
Hadrien Croubois (OpenZeppelin): Also, you have to SLOAD, possibly, the address of the destination that you want to target. So, the idea is that EIP7702 implements this natively in the protocol.

321
00:48:40.380 --> 00:48:54.739
Hadrien Croubois (OpenZeppelin): And so, what this proposal does is propose a mechanism to use this native construction instead of doing it in the user space through a delegate code. But it behaves… you can have the same behavior today through a delegate code, it's just significantly more expensive because

322
00:48:54.740 --> 00:49:01.159
Hadrien Croubois (OpenZeppelin): There are possibly SLOAD involved, and there is a way bigger bytecode that has to be deployed, and that is replicated

323
00:49:01.200 --> 00:49:03.259
Hadrien Croubois (OpenZeppelin): Possibly millions of times on the chain.

324
00:49:10.970 --> 00:49:11.810
Ansgar Dietrichs: Sounds good.

325
00:49:13.780 --> 00:49:18.880
Ansgar Dietrichs: Any other questions? I saw Derek already answered one in chat as well.

326
00:49:22.770 --> 00:49:23.990
Ansgar Dietrichs: Anything else?

327
00:49:29.870 --> 00:49:32.820
Ansgar Dietrichs: Okay, then thank you, Derek and Hadrien.

328
00:49:33.130 --> 00:49:41.050
Ansgar Dietrichs: Next up, we have… An EIP proposed by Alex?

329
00:49:41.720 --> 00:49:44.200
Ansgar Dietrichs: 7906, are you on the call?

330
00:49:44.980 --> 00:49:48.589
Alex Forshtat: Yeah, hi. Can I share my screen? Can you hear me well?

331
00:49:49.720 --> 00:49:54.359
Ansgar Dietrichs: We can hear you, and I think you should be able to share your screen. Yes, we can see your screen.

332
00:49:55.690 --> 00:50:11.349
Alex Forshtat: Okay, great. So I'm proposing EIP 79 or 6 for inclusion. These are transaction assertions, and this, EIP will allow us to do, like, enforce transaction outcomes natively, and verify state changes, on-chain.

333
00:50:11.920 --> 00:50:24.869
Alex Forshtat: So, the definition of the problem that the EIP is trying to solve is that transaction outcomes are currently unpredictable specifically for the users, because users don't exactly control outcomes of their transactions.

334
00:50:25.130 --> 00:50:42.029
Alex Forshtat: they do control the inputs, but the inputs only form a part of what determines the outcome of the transaction. They will call the bytecode, and the bytecode will read some state, and all of this together will produce the outcome that users actually care about.

335
00:50:42.150 --> 00:50:52.889
Alex Forshtat: And I think with EIP8141 included, we got a very simple solution for that, and we can just expose the state change that happened directly.

336
00:50:53.660 --> 00:51:05.309
Alex Forshtat: So, this, like, gap in transaction visibility of the outcomes is real, and it's causing, like, a lot of, losses through hacks and user mistakes.

337
00:51:05.320 --> 00:51:21.489
Alex Forshtat: and there are entire groups of attacks that are happening frequently on Ethereum and causing, like, huge losses. The major families of attacks that we're trying to address are, like, account control takeover, where, like, people just end up transferring their entire smart account to a malicious party.

338
00:51:21.560 --> 00:51:26.880
Alex Forshtat: all the drainers and fake DEXs, or, like, swaps, like,

339
00:51:27.240 --> 00:51:38.810
Alex Forshtat: fake airdrops and things like that. People also, like, use funds to, like, aggressive MEV sometimes, because the transactions they approved, allow, like, very lenient slippages or, like, price impact.

340
00:51:39.020 --> 00:51:52.900
Alex Forshtat: And also front-running, where, like, the expected contract behavior can change before the user's transaction settles. And this can, like, even target the wallets that exist today, and they try to simulate what the outcome of the transaction will be.

341
00:51:53.680 --> 00:52:07.049
Alex Forshtat: And there are, like, major incidents, like, the most famous, probably, is Bybit, which is, like, $1.5 billion, where, like, the safe was transferred, due to, like, people not being able to understand what will happen with the transaction.

342
00:52:07.190 --> 00:52:16.869
Alex Forshtat: But there are many more, and they're often, like, chains… supply chain attacks, where, like, the legitimate sites or, like, underlying infrastructure is compromised.

343
00:52:17.920 --> 00:52:27.560
Alex Forshtat: And, like, the mitigations that exist today are… like, there are two major groups, I would say. Like, one is this, mentioned, like, outcome simulation.

344
00:52:27.770 --> 00:52:39.219
Alex Forshtat: Whereas a wallet does some, like, API request to its own backend, or, like, some third-party backend, and tries to, like, simulate a transaction and estimate what will happen when this transaction lands on-chain.

345
00:52:39.410 --> 00:52:49.710
Alex Forshtat: But, like, this simulation clearly runs against, the current state. Probably. It cannot… it's impossible to simulate every possible state that can exist.

346
00:52:49.790 --> 00:53:01.810
Alex Forshtat: And if the transaction in the simulation is unreliable on itself, it can be its own threat vector, because, like, users trust the simulations, but this is not what is necessarily happening.

347
00:53:01.950 --> 00:53:12.070
Alex Forshtat: And there are, like, problems with it, because, like, this is some backend service that has to provide this, so it may not support some chains, some Devs, or, like, refuse service to users.

348
00:53:12.280 --> 00:53:23.799
Alex Forshtat: So it's not standardized, it's, like, proprietary ad hoc solution, and it kind of needs you to just give this transaction to this, like, third party for the simulation, and you get the best effort simulation.

349
00:53:24.510 --> 00:53:43.459
Alex Forshtat: The second approach is, like, on-chain, like, guard modules for smart accounts that try to run this, like, check after execution type of, assertion on-chain, and multiple, like, smart account modules do that. And the problems here is that without, like, native EVM support.

350
00:53:43.640 --> 00:53:46.849
Alex Forshtat: Like, these modules are limited in what they can do.

351
00:53:47.060 --> 00:53:59.920
Alex Forshtat: Because they cannot, express, what we call negative conditions, which means, like, nothing happened except for the thing that, like, we want to happen. You cannot express this on the current EVM.

352
00:54:00.160 --> 00:54:07.820
Alex Forshtat: You cannot observe state changes, if they are not shown by the contract as a public view function.

353
00:54:07.930 --> 00:54:11.190
Alex Forshtat: So, for example, proxies are not visible.

354
00:54:11.310 --> 00:54:14.509
Alex Forshtat: Like, proxy implementation addresses are usually not visible.

355
00:54:14.690 --> 00:54:25.359
Alex Forshtat: And, like, even if it was visible, you would… you cannot trust it, because if the proxy was taken over, then it will lie to you in its, like, Get implementation function.

356
00:54:25.650 --> 00:54:36.860
Alex Forshtat: And, like, even if you say that I just want to check that my assets are not moved, some asset types, like NFT tokens, it would be not feasible to iterate over all possible assets that you have.

357
00:54:37.010 --> 00:54:43.299
Alex Forshtat: So the guard modules are, like, good, but it is not the full, safety for users.

358
00:54:44.100 --> 00:54:50.780
Alex Forshtat: So, I believe the real mitigation here is to give users, like, full control over the possible outcomes of their transactions.

359
00:54:50.940 --> 00:55:09.650
Alex Forshtat: Because if, you look into it, the, like, financially important outcomes of EVM transactions are usually quite simple. These are, like, native balance changes, like, most of the time it's ERC20 balance changes, and, like, almost every other situation is, like, one or a couple of storage slots written in specific contracts.

360
00:55:09.780 --> 00:55:20.009
Alex Forshtat: So we can give users the outcome control with two steps. The first part is to expose these storage diffs and balance changes into the EVM.

361
00:55:20.130 --> 00:55:29.730
Alex Forshtat: And the second is to create the tooling for the wallets and, like, off-chain, like, programs that can interpret what these storage changes even mean.

362
00:55:30.770 --> 00:55:41.350
Alex Forshtat: So, the EIP7906 that I'm proposing for inclusion, it is the way to expose these balances to our changes. So, it consists of three opcodes.

363
00:55:41.570 --> 00:55:47.539
Alex Forshtat: TX trace, txDiff, and to work with events, it has event data copy.

364
00:55:47.800 --> 00:55:53.740
Alex Forshtat: And together, they expose absolutely every state change that can happen on Ethereum in the EVM.

365
00:55:54.070 --> 00:55:58.049
Alex Forshtat: And we use, like, EIP8141.

366
00:55:58.220 --> 00:56:01.360
Alex Forshtat: To get access to this opcodes.

367
00:56:01.460 --> 00:56:21.029
Alex Forshtat: to a specific frame. We introduce a new frame mode, which is, like, called post-Traction, and this frame has to run after the transaction finalizes. It cannot be included anywhere in between, and these opcodes don't work outside of this frame. So, like, there… it does not allow in Transaction introspection, you cannot, like.

368
00:56:21.190 --> 00:56:25.629
Alex Forshtat: Suddenly make decisions based on other storage rights.

369
00:56:25.870 --> 00:56:40.750
Alex Forshtat: And the only thing that it can do, it can decide to revert the entire execution body of the transaction. So every state change, that happened in the transaction reverse means that Smart Account, like, has to decide, like, and accept the finalization

370
00:56:40.930 --> 00:56:43.049
Alex Forshtat: Of the observed state change.

371
00:56:43.700 --> 00:56:51.949
Alex Forshtat: So this is what the, like, sequence diagram for this would look like. The first four steps are equivalent to how French Transaction would look like right now.

372
00:56:51.950 --> 00:57:08.740
Alex Forshtat: There are some changes at validations, and the last frame can make a call to the enforcer of transaction assertions, and this is an all-seeing frame that can observe directly into the state changes that happened throughout the transaction.

373
00:57:08.740 --> 00:57:10.920
Alex Forshtat: It makes this decision to revert or not.

374
00:57:11.390 --> 00:57:13.940
Alex Forshtat: So looking into the opcode.

375
00:57:14.260 --> 00:57:22.719
Alex Forshtat: There are three, because they provide a different splice of information, so TX trace, exposes the state change as an iterator.

376
00:57:22.830 --> 00:57:26.290
Alex Forshtat: And it very much follows the shape of a T experiment.

377
00:57:26.450 --> 00:57:34.400
Alex Forshtat: And crucially, if something is not present in a TXTrace result, it means that this change did not happen in the current transaction.

378
00:57:34.520 --> 00:57:40.589
Alex Forshtat: This is what allows us to express negative conditions, like, nothing changes, or, like, nothing else changes.

379
00:57:40.960 --> 00:57:48.109
Alex Forshtat: TXD gives you direct access to specific variables and storage slots of every contract on-chain.

380
00:57:48.250 --> 00:58:01.619
Alex Forshtat: And it can look into things that did not change and act as an alias for a SLOT, basically. And it is necessary because if you only had an iterator, it would be kind of complex to reach the specific slots.

381
00:58:01.940 --> 00:58:15.760
Alex Forshtat: And event data copy exposes, like, the data from events, for, protocols that may, like, be event… and they're even, like, like, meta-transaction relays, bundlers, and, like, cross-chain bridges, things like that.

382
00:58:16.190 --> 00:58:30.180
Alex Forshtat: So the perfect user experience that we can achieve from accepting EIP 7906 and the surrounding tooling is to make the transaction simulations feel like… and actually be enforced.

383
00:58:30.570 --> 00:58:37.719
Alex Forshtat: So, the user confirmation includes all the financially important state changes that can happen in the transaction.

384
00:58:37.860 --> 00:58:52.889
Alex Forshtat: And users can control them per transaction, and users also can install, like, persistent post-transaction policies in their smart account wallets. And basically, any violation of this policy is a guaranteed on-chain revert.

385
00:58:55.260 --> 00:59:05.120
Alex Forshtat: So, the tooling that is required to do that, most of it already exists today, it is just not joined into a single protocol.

386
00:59:05.440 --> 00:59:19.909
Alex Forshtat: So, there is a clear sign-in work group, and it is a group that's working on creating a registry of all verifying known contracts and their, like, metadata descriptions, specifically for clear signing in ERC 7730.

387
00:59:20.170 --> 00:59:37.899
Alex Forshtat: And, like, Sourceify is, of course, working on verifying all contract source codes, and Solidity already has a storage layout flag that allows you to export the storage layout definitions. And, Get has all the tracing support to collect the storage changes that happen in Transaction.

388
00:59:38.520 --> 00:59:48.920
Alex Forshtat: And now, like, there are multiple, like, kind of debuggers, like, for example, Tenderly can perfectly show you what variable changed based on the transaction trace.

389
00:59:49.250 --> 00:59:54.580
Alex Forshtat: So, like, by connecting all these tools, we can get this, like, outcome clear signing protocol.

390
00:59:55.460 --> 01:00:09.430
Alex Forshtat: So, like, to talk briefly about the impact, it kind of makes sense that the most… the first adoption and the perfect customer for this are, like, sophisticated institutional vaults and, like, projects dealing with blockchain security.

391
01:00:09.470 --> 01:00:17.489
Alex Forshtat: Because, like, we are closing here the time of check, time of use gap for existing simulations and all the policies based on the simulation.

392
01:00:17.690 --> 01:00:23.120
Alex Forshtat: We kind of collapsed the pre-execution simulation and on-chain enforcement as a single stack.

393
01:00:23.390 --> 01:00:31.899
Alex Forshtat: And yeah, of course, they can, implement policies like ban any unexpected change unless they have signed off on the allowed change.

394
01:00:32.060 --> 01:00:39.749
Alex Forshtat: So this can help them defend against attackers who know exactly what the stack they're using right now.

395
01:00:39.950 --> 01:00:48.660
Alex Forshtat: And, like, existing solutions, like the safeguards, they get, like, superpowers from adopting, like, transaction assertions natively.

396
01:00:49.740 --> 01:01:07.840
Alex Forshtat: There are a couple of questions that are often asked, like, one is, like, if tax rates is too much introspection, like gas and origin, and I believe that it's not the case because we have frame transactions, and because we can limit use of this opcode to the final static frame mode.

397
01:01:08.080 --> 01:01:16.539
Alex Forshtat: And guarantees that the only, like, decision you can make based on this new knowledge is if you revert or not, do not revert this transaction.

398
01:01:16.890 --> 01:01:28.750
Alex Forshtat: And the important thing is, with 81.41 implemented, 1706 is very simple. Like, the state diff that you need to expose, like, all nodes already have to collect it.

399
01:01:29.030 --> 01:01:32.520
Alex Forshtat: They just need to export it with this frame.

400
01:01:33.060 --> 01:01:44.699
Alex Forshtat: And, like, if there is a… it's possible that some contracts cannot be supported by this off-chain tooling if they do very custom things with their search.

401
01:01:44.770 --> 01:01:59.339
Alex Forshtat: But this should be very rare, and, like, I believe on day one, the vast majority of contracts that are actually financially meaningful can be supported, and, like, we can try to cover the long tail, down the road.

402
01:01:59.610 --> 01:02:04.559
Alex Forshtat: And, I have a couple of links to share, like, this is already implemented by ethrex.

403
01:02:04.690 --> 01:02:12.900
Alex Forshtat: I would like to invite them to speak, if they are present on the call about the implementation. They have a public DevNet that is running this EIP.

404
01:02:13.080 --> 01:02:31.139
Alex Forshtat: I have built a, like, quick playground demo of how it should look like to use Ethereum with EIP7906, and I've, like, wrote about the off-chain tooling and the clear sign-in aspect of it on Twitter on this link, if anyone's interested.

405
01:02:31.320 --> 01:02:32.709
Alex Forshtat: Thank you.

406
