01:07:10.670 --> 01:07:15.379
Kevaundray Wedderburn: I will first paste another link in.

444
01:07:16.050 --> 01:07:33.489
Kevaundray Wedderburn: Where, essentially, we analyzed 99% of the blocks to see the precompile usage, and figured out that around 50% of the precompiles are barely being used, with, like, some of them, like Blake2F, being essentially used by one contract.

445
01:07:35.180 --> 01:07:38.270
Kevaundray Wedderburn: And yeah, so the issue of this…

446
01:07:38.500 --> 01:07:54.989
Kevaundray Wedderburn: Sorry, yeah, the issue of this is that for ZKVMs, each precompile implies an accelerator or a circuit, which essentially just means the ZKVM needs to have special logic or dedicated circuits to make sure that these precompiles are fast.

447
01:07:55.550 --> 01:08:01.710
Kevaundray Wedderburn: So it increases the attack surface, or the surface that we need to audit for ZKVMs.

448
01:08:02.210 --> 01:08:05.490
Kevaundray Wedderburn: A lesser motivation, which,

449
01:08:05.630 --> 01:08:14.030
Kevaundray Wedderburn: pre-ZKVMs is that once you EVMify a precompile, you can essentially remove the native library that you have.

450
01:08:16.100 --> 01:08:23.989
Kevaundray Wedderburn: And the way to do it is, essentially, we set the code of the precompile… at the precompile address for some

451
01:08:24.120 --> 01:08:26.320
Kevaundray Wedderburn: equivalent EVM bytecode.

452
01:08:29.340 --> 01:08:33.200
Kevaundray Wedderburn: It's not super difficult, so that's about it.

453
01:08:37.660 --> 01:08:38.380
Ansgar Dietrichs: Okay.

454
01:08:39.590 --> 01:08:41.850
Ansgar Dietrichs: There's a question in chat from Toni?

455
01:08:43.819 --> 01:08:51.809
Kevaundray Wedderburn: What is the difference to just repricing the precompiles when we roll out ZK EVMs?

456
01:08:52.629 --> 01:08:58.039
Kevaundray Wedderburn: So if you reprice… so the difference here is that you'll be pricing it based on the opcode.

457
01:08:58.209 --> 01:09:01.319
